<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title><%= vuln.name %> - Vuln-World</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #f9fafb; color: #374151; line-height: 1.6; }
    header { background: #1f2937; color: #fff; padding: 15px 30px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }
    header a { color: #9ca3af; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold; }
    header a:hover { color: white; }
    .container { max-width: 900px; margin: 40px auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); }
    h1 { margin-top: 0; color: #111827; }
    h2 { color: #1f2937; border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb; padding-bottom: 10px; margin-top: 40px; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 18px; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
    .badge { display: inline-block; padding: 4px 8px; background: #e5e7eb; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold; margin-right: 10px; color: #4b5563; }
    .btn { display: inline-block; padding: 12px 24px; background: #2563eb; color: white; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: bold; margin: 20px 0; }
    .btn:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }
    pre { background: #1f2937; color: #f8f8f2; padding: 20px; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; }
    .vuln-code { border-left: 4px solid #ef4444; }
    .sec-code { border-left: 4px solid #10b981; }
    .diagram { background: #f3f4f6; padding: 20px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; }
    ul { padding-left: 20px; }
    li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <a href="/vulnerabilities">← Back to Vulnerabilities</a>
  </header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1><%= vuln.name %></h1>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.category %></span>
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.difficulty %></span>
    </div>
    
    <a href="/labs/<%= vuln.lab %>?challenge=<%= vuln.id %>" class="btn">Start Lab (<%= vuln.lab %>)</a>
    
    <h2>What Is It?</h2>
    <p>Password Reset vulnerabilities happen when the mechanism for resetting a forgotten password can be exploited. This might involve generating a reset link using a predictable token, leaking the token to a third party (like via the Referer header), or allowing Host header injection to send the user a malicious reset link.</p>

    <h2>In Simple Words</h2>
    <p>Imagine losing the key to your house. You ask a locksmith to make a new one, but he decides that the new key should just be shaped like the letters of your name. Anyone who knows your name can now forge a key to your house.</p>

    <h2>Where Does It Appear?</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Forgot Password features.</li>
        <li>Account recovery workflows.</li>
        <li>Email verification steps.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Does It Occur?</h2>
    <div class="diagram">
[Attacker] --(Requests Reset for Admin)--> [Server]
[Server]   --(Generates predictable token: md5(admin))--> [Database]
[Attacker] --(Computes md5(admin))--> [Attacker]
[Attacker] --(Submits token & new password)--> [Server]
[Server]   --(Updates password)--> [Attacker owns account]
    </div>

    <h2>HTTP Example</h2>
    <pre>
POST /reset-password HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

token=21232f297a57a5a743894a0e4a801fc3&newPassword=hacked
    </pre>

    <h2>Burp Suite Investigation</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Request a password reset for a user account you control.</li>
        <li>Observe the token you receive in your email.</li>
        <li>Attempt to figure out how it was generated (e.g., Base64 encode, timestamp, MD5 hash of username).</li>
        <li>Request a reset for a target user (e.g., admin), compute the theoretical token, and submit it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How Developers Accidentally Create It</h2>
    <p>Vulnerable Code:</p>
    <pre class="vuln-code">
// Using MD5 of the username as a reset token
const token = crypto.createHash('md5').update(user.username).digest('hex');
user.resetToken = token;
    </pre>
    <p>Secure Code:</p>
    <pre class="sec-code">
// Using a cryptographically secure random token generator
const token = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex');
user.resetToken = token;
user.tokenExpiry = Date.now() + 3600000; // 1 hour expiration
    </pre>

    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Complete account takeover.</li>
        <li>Compromise of administrator accounts.</li>
        <li>Data breaches resulting from unauthorized access.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Find It in Real Applications</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Generate multiple reset tokens for the same account and look for patterns.</li>
        <li>Test for Host Header Injection in the reset request to see if the email link domain changes.</li>
        <li>Check if the reset token leaks in HTTP Referer headers when clicking links on the reset page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Remediation</h2>
    <p>Use cryptographically secure pseudo-random number generators (CSPRNG) for reset tokens. Tokens should be long (e.g., 256 bits), single-use, and expire after a short period (e.g., 15-60 minutes). Do not use predictable elements like usernames, emails, or timestamps.</p>

    <h2>Secure Design Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Are reset tokens long, random, and unpredictable?</li>
        <li>Do reset tokens expire after a short time?</li>
        <li>Are reset tokens invalidated immediately after use?</li>
        <li>Is the application protected against Host Header Injection for the email link generation?</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Lab Investigation Guide</h2>
    <p>Try requesting a password reset for the normal user. What does the token look like? Is there a mathematical relationship between the username and the token? Once you spot the pattern, trigger a reset for the 'admin' and forge the correct token.</p>

    <h2>Post-Lab Explanation</h2>
    <p>The application generated password reset tokens by simply computing the MD5 hash of the username. When an attacker requested a password reset for 'admin', the application stored `md5("admin")` in the database as the valid token. The attacker could then independently calculate this MD5 hash and use it to successfully change the admin's password.</p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
